up our own  essay and actually we tried quite a few  things because we really wanted this  essay to be  relatively quick fast relatively simple  to set up  especially since we uh we were really  busy with uh doing uh thousands and  thousands of kobe tests so we tried  first the direct sample approach with no  instruction at all and putting the  sample directly into the pcr master mix  and this obviously was very fast but it  was really not quite as sensitive as we  wanted it to be  we tried a few a couple of few um  off-board extractions and um of course  this is not a very extensive list we  decided that this is was also not really  adding a whole lot to the sensitivity  and the sample just didn't seem to lie  sufficiently with the methods we tried  but we could have tried a few more  things of course but then the fungal  lysis buffer came available available  from uh dinosaur and so we tried this  and it actually proved to be uh the  winning ticket um  the fungal lysis buffer allows us to  basically lines the sample ahead of time  by adding uh some uh volume of the  sample with  about one tenth of the volume with um of  lysis buffer uh the  sample pretty much cooks uh for 16  minutes it's 30 minutes at 60 degrees  centigrade where it lies quite  efficiently is then in deactivated at 95  degrees centigrade for 10 minutes and  then it's basically ready to be set up  in amplification uh where uh we're using  uh for  many different real-time pcr instruments  could be using used here uh we're using  the diastoring lysosome mdx and the 96  well  direct  sample  plate  using two microliters of samples and  eight microliters of master mix  so  as again as i said we we tried it a few  things but this seemed to be working  pretty well and i'm showing you uh here  some just some examples of results where  you can see the amplification curves i  have four samples here to our patients  and to our controls and you can see that  the amplification curves uh of the first  patient is just purple that's just the  internal control the amplification curve  of the second patient is actually both  the green and the purple curve  indicating amplification of  uh the target for sea auris as well and  these are the negative and the positive  control  um  so after we uh we finish with sort of  this um  finding out what the best conditions  were or we could do our validation  studies to be able to bring this test uh  live in-house uh as i'm sure a lot of  the laboratories here in the audience  are familiar validation studies of  course include accuracy precision  sensitivity and specificity for accuracy  um  i must say was relatively easy for us  because we were already sending the test  out we were already swabbing a lot of  patients and sending them out to the  tennessee lab so we basically compared  it  compared our pcr to the tennessee lab  pcr and also we had some reference  material and known concentration that we  could also use  for precision we wanted to see how  reproducible uh the study was  over different days and different um  technologies and we looked at  specifically at the coefficient  values for uh or reproducibility  uh for uh looking at the city values of  the positive control  for sensitivity we were able to get  tighter uh  positive cultures at tighter  concentration and dilute those down and  do our limited detection studies and for  specificity we looked at the performance  of the essay against different types of  candida  and  other types of organisms as well and the  results were really good um and with 100  percent accuracy really good  reproducibility and a limited detection  that we could  prove to be two colony forming units per  reaction  um so we were able to  go live with this test  and introduce it in-house at the  beginning of this year  that was a major win  because it allowed us to for much more  rapid results of course  and um we were able to now introduce the  test uh in the  emr  allow the infection prevention team to  with just a few clicks um identify the  patients put them in isolation and order  both the culture and the pcr we decided  to go live with both tests being done on  the same swab again collecting the  axilla groin one swab and setting up  with the same swap both tests  this allowed for much faster results and  everybody seemed to be very happy that  we were able to do this including the  lab who had been had to delay the  project  for a while  so the volume of testing has increased  over time and  this is showing  real data from when we went live to  pretty much last month  at one point we decided we wanted to  really look at the concordance between  the pcr  and and the culture because we really  were doing a lot of work and a lot of  screening and we wanted to see how much  value the um  the culture was really adding to this  um so we um  compared it and saw that um  for um  the culture we actually had four  patients who are positive by pcr but  negative by culture and in a way this  was sort of expected  and we were able to  confirm that these cases were actually  proven to be true positive based on  their history or subsequent cultures  that were then positive  on the other hand though the culture was  not really identifying any  additional positives there were zero  that were pcr negative and culture  positive so we decided to bring this up  to our collaborators we tend to make  decisions here when it comes to  screening especially in other tests in  the lab really in a collaborative  fashion so we discussed this in our  infectious disease collaborative with  the physicians and as well as infection  prevention and we decided that it was  really time to stop  doing both cultures and pcr and so we  discontinued that in august and are  proceeding just with a pcr test as our  screening test  today we have screened over 1300  patients and i'm sure we will continue  to do so and probably increase the  screening even further  um it is  a daunting task to have one more test  with such high volume  however we feel this is really important  for for the system  uh we also really keep a very close eye  on the turnaround time of the results  because obviously these patients  are in isolation until proven uh  not guilty so to speak and so we really  keep a  really close eye on the data and here  i'm showing the volume of testing in  each month and you can see that it's  going up and down but mostly up  um in some cases there are some events  that prompt more screening and i'm  showing you the turnaround time from the  in-lab  stamp to the final result the verified  results which is important but um also  as important and more important is the  collect to verify turnaround time which  hovers around 30 hours um  at the moment we're batching once a day  and uh we're not doing uh much testing  over the weekend but we're already  thinking that we want to do a little bit  better than that and probably we start  testing heavily on the weekend as well  and keep in mind that we're doing this  in a central lab location uh so there's  some transportation for some of our  samples from coming from our hospitals  we are uh the radius of the daycare  health system is is pretty wide in terms  of mile and transportation so at one  point we may need to  decentralize this test and put it closer  to the patients in the hospital labs  we're not quite ready to do that now but  something definitely that we need to  keep in mind  another important thing  to consider is the fact that we need to  give better tools to our  clinical teams to identify patients so  we cannot just rely on sexual prevention  identifying these patients but we need  to uh  make the emergency department the er  team in charge of identifying these  patients when they first come into the  hospital so now we've introduced  other tools called the power plan where  questions are being asked as at the  triage whether the patients have had a  stay in a long term acute care hospital  or they're coming from a facility that  has known cases of the oris and there's  active doh departmental health  communication on a list of  facilities that have those so if the  questions are answered yes  then the actual the pcr test is actually  ordered automatically so that we don't  miss any potential colonized patients  so having said all of this i want to  conclude um  with a few thoughts uh  i think i've imparted on all of you that  candida ores is a fungus that has very  very serious consequences and is  spreading in the healthcare facilities  and in our in the us as well as the rest  of the world  um accurate identification of this  organism was not always possible in the  past however new methodologies are  available new software so accurate  identification of both infections in the  positive culture as well as colonies  patients is now possible  specifically rapid identification of  colonized patients is really necessary  and needed to prevent hospital-based  transmissions  molecular based methods are really ideal  for screening because there's they're  fast  and they're  very convenient  and uh of course they're it's even  better when they're available in house  and the last thought i have is that  really for all these projects  as we have learned through covet but  also through uh with this specific  project on candida or is that  inter-departmental collaboration is  really vital to make things successful  um really lab cannot work in a silos and  so we work very very closely with  infection prevention infectious disease  specialists and the hospital staff  and on that note um i want to thank my  collaborators at daycare on this project  the lab supervisor who did  really spearheaded all the validation  studies uh the technical director dr  ceferi the technical director of  microbiology the director of infection  prevention suzanne chandler  dr gupta who's the quality and project  improvement director  and of course thank you  also to the dinosauring molecular team  thank you maura for your informative  presentation  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor dinosaur and molecular  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions submitted today and during the  on-demand period will be addressed by  the speaker via the contact information  you provided at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand lab  routes will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event  until next time goodbye
 

